  "body": "Firefox is more strict than Chrome about XSL errors, and it's unclear to me if that is something we wish to act  upon.\r\n\r\nFor instance, Firefox throws an error when `<xsl:stylesheet>` is missing a `version` attribute, or when an `xsl:output method` is not `\"text\"`, `\"html\"`, or `\"xml\"`. Chrome seems to ignore the missing `version` and go ahead with `1.0`, and also ignore the `output` method entirely in favor of just transforming as `html` no matter what value is actually given.\r\n\r\nI'm personally unaware of any actual webcompat fallout from this, but I wouldn't be surprised if it is a pain point for developers, as I recently spoke with a dev on Reddit about Firefox throwing unclear errors (in their case they happened to specify \"xhtml\" as an output method, which Chrome happily accepted, but Firefox reported an error).\r\n\r\nSo if XSL is not removed from the web platform anytime soon, it might be worth checking if and how implementations should align on these kinds of cases.",
